WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.589
JavaScript and TypeScript applications still need runtime validation because external data arrives at runtime.

2
00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:20.185
TypeScript can help developers reason about code, but it does not prove that a request body, query parameter, route parameter, header, cookie, or webhook payload matches the expected shape.

3
00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:27.226
JSON parsing only proves that data is parseable, not that it is safe, authorized, complete, or meaningful.

4
00:00:27.226 --> 00:00:34.580
Schema validation gives teams a clear way to define expected fields, types, formats, ranges, and constraints.

5
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:41.465
It can reject unknown fields, normalize allowed values, and keep invalid data away from business logic.

6
00:00:41.465 --> 00:00:55.547
This is especially important in APIs where clients may send extra fields, nested objects, unexpected arrays, or values that look harmless until they reach a database query, file operation, or downstream service.

7
00:00:55.547 --> 00:00:59.537
Type confusion and mass assignment are common design risks.

8
00:00:59.537 --> 00:01:05.874
Type confusion happens when code assumes data has one type or shape but receives something else.

9
00:01:05.874 --> 00:01:17.296
Mass assignment happens when user-controlled fields can set properties that should not be user-controlled, such as roles, ownership, approval status, or internal flags.

10
00:01:17.296 --> 00:01:23.477
Validation should define both what is allowed and what must never be accepted from the client.

11
00:01:23.477 --> 00:01:31.222
Validation should happen before data reaches business logic, databases, files, commands, or downstream services.

12
00:01:31.222 --> 00:01:34.664
Error responses should be useful but not revealing.

13
00:01:34.664 --> 00:01:44.835
A client can be told that input failed validation without receiving stack traces, internal schema details, database messages, or implementation paths.

14
00:01:44.835 --> 00:01:50.781
Good validation improves security, reliability, and developer clarity at the same time.
